WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.003 position:56%
It hurts. It's unbearable sometimes.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.538
There have been many, many times
I've thought, I'm just quitting.

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.208
There have been so many occasions
when I've walked onto the ward

00:00:08.208 --> 00:00:10.643
and it has been unsafe
because of the levels of staffing.

00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:13.546
You feel devalued,
you feel a little bit worthless.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.849 position:56%
You feel like you're not appreciated
for what you do.

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:28.695
Adele is a junior doctor
in the UK's National Health Service

00:00:28.695 --> 00:00:31.598
who walked out of her hospital
in a four-day strike in April,

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:35.769
which led to more than
195,000 missed appointments.

00:00:35.769 --> 00:00:38.538
Claps don’t pay the bills,
claps don’t pay the bills.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.607
In the first half of 2023,

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:44.010
the number of canceled appointments
amounted to almost half a million

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.380
as the medical sector took part
in the UK's union actions.

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:50.850 position:61%
Over the past year, the UK has recorded
the largest number of strikes

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:55.789 position:64%
since the 1980s, with sectors from health
care to postal service workers

00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.124 position:66%
and teachers all taking industrial action.

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:00.827
It is not a coincidence
that they come at a time

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:04.798 position:66%
when the cost of living crisis
is cutting into the pockets of the nation,

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:08.535
with the cost of basic necessities
rocketing to new levels.

00:01:12.872 --> 00:01:15.008
Industrial action is having
a serious impact

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:17.077
on the quality
of patient care.

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:19.045
Last week’s strike
by junior doctors caused

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:22.849 position:56%
the cancelation of almost
200,000 operations and appointments.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:26.252 position:66%
The only thing we can really do is strike,
and the only cause

00:01:26.252 --> 00:01:28.955
we can really legitimately strike
about is our pay.

00:01:28.955 --> 00:01:31.357
But it's sort of an indicator
for loads of other stuff.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:35.128
Junior doctors are asking for a
35% pay increase.

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:38.698
This round of pay negotiations
reflects both the acute squeeze

00:01:38.698 --> 00:01:42.635 position:56%
created by high inflation now
and more than a decade of inadequate

00:01:42.635 --> 00:01:44.938
funding that has seen
public sector workers

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:48.141 position:56%
fall way behind their private sector
counterparts.

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:52.812 position:67%
So, junior doctors, their pay has increased
by about 16% in cash terms since 2008.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:55.615
But in that same period, inflation
and other factors mean that

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:59.385 position:64%
they’re actually 25% worse off,
and they’re just saying enough is enough.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.188
I've been a junior doctor
for four years now.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:05.291
I'm doing a ten-month long
contract in plastic surgery

00:02:05.291 --> 00:02:08.328
just to get the experience
I need in order to try

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:11.731
and make a successful application
for my actual training post.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:15.168 position:63%
My current job has a higher base salary,

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:18.538
so it's about £40,000,
but I never do night shifts.

00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:22.709
The extra hours, the night shifts,
they really do make up

00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.110
the bulk of our salaries.

00:02:24.110 --> 00:02:28.481 position:66%
So when you factor all that together, I've
actually taken a pretty decent pay cut.

00:02:29.682 --> 00:02:31.718
I will usually be scheduled

00:02:31.718 --> 00:02:36.289
to work with one of the consultants
in the operating theaters.

00:02:36.289 --> 00:02:39.425 position:61%
Within plastic surgery, there's loads
of different things you could be doing.

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:41.227
There's hand surgeries.

00:02:41.227 --> 00:02:42.695
You can do breast surgery,

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:44.464
so it's a lot of cancer surgery.

00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.199
Plastic surgery includes burns.

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.201 position:56%
So you might be doing skin grafting.

00:02:48.201 --> 00:02:52.172
Plastic surgeons can do cleft lip
and palate surgery.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:55.775
They can do head and neck surgery,
which is largely taking tumors.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.410
At the moment, I'm on-call.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:58.878
I started my on-call shift

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:01.848
on Saturday and I will go straight
through until this Friday.

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:05.051
And during those shifts
we then have to do trauma clinics.

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:08.855
So there's an awful lot of strain
within the NHS,

00:03:08.855 --> 00:03:12.325
and the more people are struggling,

00:03:12.325 --> 00:03:15.061
the more people are burning out.

00:03:15.061 --> 00:03:18.865
Since I started work it’s
not been uncommon

00:03:18.865 --> 00:03:20.500
to hear someone say that

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.802
one of their colleagues
has committed suicide.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:27.507 position:70%
According to the British Medical Association,
around seven in ten

00:03:27.507 --> 00:03:32.612 position:64%
junior doctors surveyed say they always
or frequently work in understaffed rotas,

00:03:32.612 --> 00:03:35.415
which may be placing
patient and staff safety at risk.

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:39.886 position:64%
Around half of junior doctors
described their desire to work in the NHS

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:43.756 position:56%
in the next year as low or very low.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.692 position:58%
When you first move into a new house,

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:49.195 position:59%
all sorts of things spring up
that you weren't expecting to pay for.

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:52.532
When I started my new job,
there was a mistake made

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:55.868 position:59%
with my pay and it's taken five months
to get that resolved.

00:03:55.868 --> 00:04:01.608 position:66%
I've been consistently worrying
about money, consistently in my overdraft.

00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:05.378
If any unexpected thing happens,
you always feel

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:09.315 position:58%
just a heartbeat away
from absolute disaster, to be honest.

00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:12.218
So when it comes to pay in the NHS,
the government says that it’s set

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:15.555 position:75%
by these independent peer review bodies,
which they say they can't really interfere with.

00:04:15.555 --> 00:04:16.556
These peer review bodies,

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:18.291
they will gather evidence
over the course of the year,

00:04:18.291 --> 00:04:21.828 position:72%
they give their recommendation
for maybe a two, three, five percent pay rise,

00:04:21.828 --> 00:04:24.030
and although, you know,
these strikes are directed

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.231
at the government,
the government is trying to,

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:28.134
take their hands out of it
and say it's not really our deal.

00:04:28.134 --> 00:04:29.469
At the same time,

00:04:29.469 --> 00:04:32.005 position:63%
we're getting in a bit of an impasse
right now. Junior doctors are asking for

00:04:32.005 --> 00:04:35.975 position:63%
what's called full pay restoration,
which would be a 35% pay rise right now.

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:37.710
The government is saying
that is completely unaffordable

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.712
and unacceptable
and they have to lower that.

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.549
You know, there are some things
that you see at work

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:47.720
or that you do at work
that if you were to say them

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:51.724
to a person who doesn't
work in health care,

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:55.295
they just wouldn't understand
why you're upset.

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.596
Did you work last night?

00:04:56.596 --> 00:04:58.965
Yeah. So this is night
three of four for me.

00:04:58.965 --> 00:05:03.102 position:67%
So I'm slightly delirious,
but it’s OK, we're going to get through it.

00:05:03.102 --> 00:05:06.472 position:56%
So I think pay is a difficult issue.

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:10.209
On bank holidays, the person
in the hospital paid the least is

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:13.980
the F1, so the junior doctor
who's been a doctor for a year.

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:19.018 position:56%
So you get paid more in the hospital
to clean toilets on a bank holiday.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:22.889
It hasn't been safe for many years
and I don't think that will come

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.223
as a shock to anyone.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:27.226
There is nowhere else
that a junior doctor can work.

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:29.462 position:61%
We can't even work in private hospitals

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:31.764
because the reason people go
private is to ensure that

00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:33.499
they get seen by a consultant.

00:05:33.499 --> 00:05:37.770 position:56%
So really our only options are ditch
the NHS entirely.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:40.907
A lot of people have moved
to places like Australia

00:05:40.907 --> 00:05:44.610
and New Zealand, where you are
valued more highly.

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:47.146
About two out of five
doctors said in a recent poll

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:50.083
that they are looking to leave the
NHS or would accept another job

00:05:50.083 --> 00:05:51.117
if that was proposed.

00:05:51.117 --> 00:05:52.719
And 80% are actually
saying that

00:05:52.719 --> 00:05:54.687
they are constantly thinking
about ways out as well.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:59.359 position:56%
The ability to continue striking
is what's proving difficult because,

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:02.562 position:56%
you know, we're striking
because we are not being paid enough

00:06:02.562 --> 00:06:05.932
and we therefore don't
really have the ability

00:06:05.932 --> 00:06:08.868
to take that much unpaid leave
from our work.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:12.305 position:63%
So I think the longer the strikes go
on, the more difficult it's going to be.

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:13.973
But I think the majority of people

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:16.142
have reached the point
where they are going to do anything

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:20.213 position:63%
they have to in order to be able to
continue striking and making that point.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.415
The fact that people are
still willing to fight

00:06:22.415 --> 00:06:26.419
tells me that people have hope
that things will get better.

